Prepared shader bindings and generated shader state
==================================================

Source and ownership
  Approved proposal: docs/Rendering.ShaderBindingState.Proposal.md.
  Audit and implementation evidence: docs/Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md.
  This list owns implementation of the approved proposal. docs/PBR.BaselineShading.todo retains
  the scheduling entry immediately after water volume absorption and in-scattering.
  Existing partial implementation is input to review, not evidence that a task below is complete.

Goal and shared constraints
  Submit fixed-slot resources through prepared executable metadata without runtime resource-name
  or uniform-location resolution. Retain non-UBO inputs in generated shader state and suppress
  redundant uploads without losing context-wide binding or resource-lifetime correctness.
  Keep one authoritative authored contract and one context-wide GPU binding cache.
  Use concrete GpuTexture subclasses for VGE-owned resources. Preserve existing raw engine-ID
  boundaries, target/sampler policies and the name and ownership contract of GBufferPosition.
  Leave UBO contents, staging, uploads and ring-buffer behavior with their existing owners.
  Preserve engine APIs and rendering output. Do not add textures or change water transport here.
  Run every build and test through subagents. GPU fixtures use offline SPIR-V and established GPU
  abstractions; no live GLSL test compilation or raw GL where an abstraction exists.
  Keep parsing/diagnostics in the contract generator, offline layouts/artifact metadata in
  ShaderBuildTool, executable inspection in SPIR-V preparation, and publication in rendering owners.
  Comment new classes/methods, group APIs by visibility and responsibility, and keep composition
  roots thin. Project-management staging language belongs only in planning documents.

Implementation decisions to resolve with evidence
  These are bounded choices within the approved design, not requests to reopen its architecture.
  ☐ Determine whether the offline compiler requires sampler/image numeric locations. If required,
    allocate them deterministically as private compiler metadata with collision checks; otherwise
    remove unnecessary qualifiers. Neither result permits authored location properties for submission.
  ☐ Identify whether existing consumers require binding aliases. Reject ambiguous overlaps unless
    an existing supported alias is explicitly represented and validated; do not add speculative aliases.
  ☐ Choose per-executable successful uniform history or conservative invalidation on variant switches.
    Record the choice and prove first-use, switching, replacement and external-write behavior.
  ☐ Inventory mutable inputs and select owned snapshots or explicit content revisions for each.
    Identify missing resource/cache lifecycle notifications before adding new tracking mechanisms.

Phase 1: Complete the contract and consumer inventory
  Outcome: every affected declaration and runtime consumer has a justified migration path.

  ☐ Expand docs/Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md into an occurrence-level audit.
    - List every UniformLocation descriptor, inline sampler/image location, and suspected extra
      uniform used for CPU bookkeeping, including shared declarations and affected shader variants.
    - Record each resource contract, current runtime consumer, actual purpose and removal/retention
      reason. Distinguish existing sampler identity metadata from previous-value storage; do not
      characterize all shared locations as redundant or invent extra shader uniforms.
    - Include ordinary standalone uniform writes, arrays, indexed engine uniform dictionaries,
      external shader APIs and any compatibility dependence on sampler/image locations.
  ☐ Inventory generated and authored properties, specialized Submit overrides and concrete graphics
    and compute owners. Identify gaps in the existing partial state generator and submission path.
    - Trace GpuProgramInterface, GpuProgramLayout, ShaderBindingSubmission, RuntimeSubmissionEmitter,
      BindingReader, ShaderBindingAttribute and ShaderBuildTool source layout/artifact consumers.
    - Record resource recreation/retirement, external engine writes and GlStateCache invalidation
      boundaries; include buffer ranges, image views, raw IDs and mutable scalar/vector/matrix inputs.
  ☐ Resolve the bounded implementation decisions above and record evidence before dependent edits.
  ☐ Gate: the audit accounts for every affected owner and declaration, and identifies compatibility
    paths needing independent review before removal. No location removal relies on blanket counts.

Phase 2: Prepare immutable bindings for each executable
  Depends on Phase 1. Outcome: validated executable metadata can drive direct slot submission.

  ☐ Extend the single authoritative contract with the metadata needed for preparation.
    - Carry kind, slot, compatible type, stage/variant applicability, required-input policy,
      target/sampler policy and array extent. Generate stable internal contract-entry indices.
    - Keep these indices distinct from GPU locations and authored binding numbers. Preserve names
      for diagnostics and offline matching without requiring SPIR-V debug names at runtime.
    - Validate offline declarations and layouts per selected variant, including missing applicable
      declarations, conflicting contracts, slot ranges, stage agreement and resource namespaces.
  ☐ Build an immutable prepared binding table in the executable preparation owner.
    - Enumerate linked sampler/image resources once; inspect type/array extent and initial unit
      assignments, then match fixed slots in the correct namespace. Inspection locations stay private.
    - Match UBOs, storage blocks and atomic-counter buffers through binding metadata; retain actual
      standalone uniform locations for value uploads. Include element-aware array metadata.
    - Reject incompatible types, invalid assignments and ambiguous overlaps. Sampler and image unit
      namespaces remain distinct. Never silently repair fixed assignments with sampler uniform writes.
  ☐ Encode resource activity separately from required-input policy.
    - Variant-excluded and legally optimized-away resources may be inactive. Missing applicable
      declarations are offline validation errors, not automatically inferred from linked inactivity.
    - Required means a valid input for an active binding, not forced compiler retention. Introduce
      an active-resource assertion only if an audited consumer needs one, with dedicated validation.
  ☐ Couple prepared-table lifetime to executable generation and variant selection.
    - Publish a replacement executable and complete table together only after successful validation.
      Preserve the previous executable/table if preparation fails; retire them through existing owners.
    - Refresh offline artifact metadata and its existing version/invalidation mechanism where necessary
      so stale generated contracts cannot be paired with incompatible binaries.
  ☐ Verify contract conflicts, types, arrays, namespace separation, excluded/optimized resources,
    failed preparation and successful replacement through generator/offline and focused GPU tests.
  ☐ Gate: each executable has validated direct-index metadata; no supported mapping depends on
    preserved debug names or an arbitrary selection among conflicting slots.

Phase 3: Submit resources through prepared slots
  Depends on Phase 2. Outcome: normal fixed-slot publication no longer resolves uniform locations.

  ☐ Change generated submission and rendering APIs to consume prepared entries directly.
    - Snapshot each source once and validate the complete active set before publishing any bindings.
    - Skip inactive entries. Reject missing/retired required active resources. Clear invalid or absent
      optional active resources through established abstractions, including buffer bindings.
    - Publish textures/images to fixed slots via GlStateCache; publish buffer resources/ranges via
      existing owners. Preserve image access, format, level/layer and engine target/sampler policies.
    - Remove the normal fixed-slot path's dependence on ActiveUniform, name/location resolution,
      TryBindSamplerTextureActive and ApplyUniformUnitContract. Keep only justified adapter consumers
      until their replacement is reviewed; do not remove ordinary uniform upload support.
  ☐ Resolve current resource validity and allocation identity even when wrapper references match.
    - Reuse owner generations/notifications and cache invalidation; cover raw ID retirement/reuse.
    - Preserve UBO owner-controlled uploads and content-only texture updates independently of binding
      reference changes. Do not add a second global binding cache or unconditional redundant queries.
  ☐ Verify offline fixtures read/write expected data at sampler/image slots without authored location
    descriptors/attributes, and verify required failure, optional clearing and inactive-resource skips.
    - Prove a failed validation publishes no partial input set; retry retains pending upload work.
    - Prove A-to-B-to-A restores shared texture/image/buffer slots and retirement cannot reuse stale data.
  ☐ Gate: fixed-slot submission performs no executable reflection or name/location lookup; a context
    cache comparison remains permitted and necessary when unchanged local state must restore a binding.

Phase 4: Complete generated state and successful uniform publication tracking
  Depends on Phase 3. Outcome: all shader owners retain correct inputs and skip redundant value work.

  ☐ Emit a <ShaderName>State struct and active instance for each participating concrete shader class.
    - Include every non-UBO value/resource contract property with its proper type; exclude descriptors
      and UBO contents. C# structs are inherently non-inheritable; do not emit a final modifier.
    - Generate getters and mutation-checked setters that compare retained values and record only
      actual changes. Preserve nullable resources and existing input-mutation lifecycle checks.
    - Sample authored getters once and compare them into the same model. Bring specialized Submit
      implementations onto the shared mechanism or document and verify an equivalent contract.
  ☐ Implement equality and change tracking for every audited property category.
    - Scalars/vectors/matrices use exact upload-appropriate comparison, never approximate suppression.
    - Managed resources compare desired identity but still validate current lifetime/allocation.
    - Image views include resource, level, layer, layered flag, access and format; buffer ranges include
      resource, offset and size. Mutable inputs use the selected snapshot or revision mechanism.
    - Raw IDs retain ownership-boundary semantics; unchanged integers do not prove unchanged lifetime.
  ☐ Track successful ordinary uniform uploads for the selected executable generation.
    - Upload on first use even when the desired value equals default state, and after actual changes.
    - Apply the selected variant-switch/replacement invalidation strategy; clearing one executable's
      dirty state must not suppress another executable's required upload.
    - Commit upload history only for successful uploads; failure and retry must not lose pending work.
    - Route supported external uniform writes through tracking or invalidate affected history.
  ☐ Preserve context-wide binding checks for retained resources regardless of local dirty state.
    - Test recreation/retirement with unchanged wrappers, engine callbacks, cache invalidation and
      content updates that need uploads but do not inherently require a resource rebind.
  ☐ Verify complete generated state, authored sources, mutable inputs, equal/changed assignments,
    first-use/default-value uploads, variant switches, replacement and failed-submission retry.
  ☐ Gate: all inventoried graphics/compute owners participate; unchanged values suppress redundant
    assignments/uploads while context switches and lifetime changes still restore valid GPU state.

Phase 5: Migrate declarations and remove reviewed compatibility dependencies
  Depends on Phases 3 and 4 and their focused validation. Outcome: authored resource contracts no
  longer carry redundant sampler/image locations, and supported engine behavior is preserved.

  ☐ Implement any required engine dictionary/API adapters using preparation-owned metadata.
    - Preserve public engine interfaces without feeding name/location lookup back into VGE submission.
    - Record every justified ordinary-uniform or compatibility location retained by the audit.
  ☐ Obtain independent review of compatibility consumers and replacement evidence before removing
    each compatibility path. Address review findings and verify affected engine-facing contracts.
  ☐ Remove audited redundant descriptors, inline locations and GLSL qualifiers as toolchain evidence
    permits. Remove ShaderBindingAttribute.UniformLocation after all its consumers migrate.
    - Remove obsolete parser/emitter/schema tests and lookup branches, replacing them with the new
      contract tests; retain required ordinary uniform support and private compiler metadata.
    - Cover liquid/composite local declarations and the complete shared inventory, not just seven
      local descriptor properties. Record an explicit justification for every retained entry.
    - Remove transitional scaffolding after dependent consumers migrate. Any surviving compatibility
      adapter must have a supported consumer and documented responsibility, not an indefinite fallback.
  ☐ Regenerate affected offline artifacts through the actual configured build target and verify no
    stale bindings survive. Run affected generator, runtime-submission and GPU regressions.
  ☐ Gate: the occurrence-level audit matches the final declarations and consumers; no authored
    sampler/image location remains solely to enable normal fixed-slot resource submission.

Phase 6: Integration evidence and final acceptance
  Depends on Phase 5. Outcome: the approved proposal is demonstrably satisfied and traceable.

  ☐ Run the required build and affected generator/runtime/GPU suites through subagents, using fresh
    offline SPIR-V artifacts. Record commands, results, skips and any unresolved failures in the audit.
    - Include fixed-slot sampling/image writes, arrays/aliases/namespaces, variant inactivity, required
      and optional semantics, uniform first-use/changes, shader switching and generation replacement.
    - Include mutable inputs, engine IDs, wrapper recreation/retirement, external writes/invalidation,
      validation-before-publication and retry. Use semantic assertions and meaningful operation counts.
  ☐ Instrument steady-state behavior and report assignment skips, preparation/reflection work,
    submission/cache checks, uniform uploads and actual GL binds separately.
    - Demonstrate no executable reflection or uniform-name/location resolution for steady-state
      fixed-slot submission, and no redundant GL operations for repeated identical bindings.
    - Do not claim zero CPU work or measured frame-time gains from setter or call-count reductions.
  ☐ Update the audit with final architecture, source references, coverage and retained compatibility.
    Reconcile this list against every approved proposal requirement and resolve coverage gaps.
  ☐ Obtain a final independent implementation review; resolve findings and record evidence separately
    from the pre-removal compatibility review. Do not treat passing builds as full completion.
  ☐ Obtain user-run live visual acceptance for affected rendering/engine boundaries. Do not launch
    the game; distinguish pending user validation from successful build/headless-test evidence.
  ☐ Gate: all proposal requirements, audit work, focused validation, independent review and live
    acceptance are satisfied. Record any unresolved work as open rather than silently deferring scope.
  ☐ Only then mark this list and the linked PBR scheduling task complete. Relocating numeric metadata
    or skipping equal field assignments alone never satisfies completion.
